\begin{note}
Not every ordering is a \emph{total} order.
For example, we could compare \lstinline{Vec}s by Pareto domination:
\lstinline{a <= b} means every component of \lstinline{a} is no larger than the corresponding component of \lstinline{b}.
Then \lstinline{Vec(1, 2)} and \lstinline{Vec(2, 1)} are incomparable:
neither is less than or equal to the other.
Python's \lstinline{sorted} does not require a total order,
only one that is consistent, and returns \emph{some} linear extension of the partial order.
\end{note}
